The PIT does not lose an IRQ 0 tick that arrives while the last one is
still pending at the 8259: it is raised again when the CPU acknowledges.

irq_timer runs when the main loop wakes, and a wakeup is late by however
coarse the host's waits are. Every transition that came due meanwhile is
raised back to back under the BQL, before the CPU can take one, and on
the edge-triggered 8259 they are one interrupt. A guest at 1 kHz --
Windows 9x's multimedia timer, which a MIDI sequencer and many games run
on -- keeps time by counting IRQ 0, so its clock ran at the rate of the
host's wakeups: 100 % on Linux, 50 % with 2 ms waits, and 6 % on a
Windows host, whose waits round to its 15.6 ms timer tick unless the
process asks otherwise (music "too slow" on the first Windows host,
2026-09-17). Measured on Linux with the waits rounded that way
(tools/wait-granularity.c) by guest-tools' PITRATE.COM, the rate phase of
tools/pit-guest-test.py: 6 % before, 100 % after, spinning and halted.

An edge that finds IRQ 0 requested and unmasked at the master PIC is
counted; pic_intack calls a hook the PIT registers, which raises one owed
tick as a fresh edge -- after the ISR is entered, so the PIC holds it until
the EOI and the handler runs once per tick. Owed ticks are capped at a
quarter second's worth and a new count drops them. A guest whose IRQ 0 is
masked at the 8259 (an APIC HAL) owes nothing.

The owed ticks are paced (2026-09-22): an acknowledge raises the next
owed edge only when half a period has passed since the last owed one, so
a catch-up runs at twice the rate -- a regular tick and an owed one per
period -- rather than as one burst. Raised at the acknowledge itself they nest -- Windows 98's
timer handler acknowledges early and runs with interrupts on -- and a
quarter second of 1 kHz ticks delivered back to back, after the vCPU had
stalled inside an MMIO access (the Direct3D executor in the Wine process
compiling its first shaders), took the VMM down with a fatal exception
0D; a 3.5 s stall reset the machine. Measured with D3D7TEST on the Win98
machine: dead as one burst, 300 frames at 57 fps paced.

-global isa-pit.reinject=off restores upstream's behaviour (the A/B).

